<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrei Bogolyubsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1111–<a href="/years/1174">1174</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Andrey Bogolyubsky, Andrei I of Vladimir · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrei Bogolyubsky was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a> and Grand Prince of Vladimir from 1157. He moved his capital from Rostov-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> to Vladimir, adorned it with white-stone churches and built a palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogolyubovo">Bogolyubovo</a>. His armies sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 1169, showing the shift of power to north-eastern Rus', and he was murdered by his own boyars in 1174.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky.jpg" alt="Andrei Bogolyubsky" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лапоть · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrey_Bogolyubsky_-_Vladimir_Historical_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, Andrey's parents married on 12 January 1108 as part of a peace between the Rus' and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. His father was Yuri Dolgoruki, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and a son of Volodimer II Monomakh, and his mother was an unnamed Cuman princess, a daughter of Aepa. Andrey was born in about 1111, and Yuri made him prince in Vyshgorod, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. He accompanied his father during a brief capture of Kiev in 1149. In 1155 he left Vyshgorod for Vladimir, a small town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klyazma">Klyazma</a>, taking with him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/icon">Icon</a> of the Blessed Mother of God, afterwards known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-vladimir">Virgin of Vladimir</a>, an action the Kievan Chronicle condemned as theft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Yuri's death in 1157, Andrey became Prince of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. In 1162 he ousted his younger brothers Mikhail and Vsevolod from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> and Suzdal, united his father's lands under his sole rule and expelled four brothers, with their mother, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. He began fortifications at Vladimir in 1158, completed in 1164, and the Dormition Cathedral. He sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> asking for a separate metropolitan see at Vladimir, but the patriarch refused. In 1164 he attacked the Volga Bolgars, and he built the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogolyubovo">Bogolyubovo</a> near Vladimir, which became his favourite residence.</p><p>In March 1169 his troops sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, plundering religious art, books and valuables and devastating houses and churches. Andrey stayed in Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. He claimed the title of Grand Prince, kept his residence at Vladimir and placed his brother Gleb on the Kievan throne, an overlordship that ended with Gleb's death on 20 January 1171. His siege of Novgorod in 1170 failed, though a later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> won the princedom there for his son Yury in 1171. In 1173 a second coalition he assembled was defeated at Vyshgorod.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The defeat at Vyshgorod, the growth of his authority and his conflicts with the boyars led to a conspiracy, and on the night of 28-29 June 1174 twenty boyars burst into his chambers and killed him in his bed. The chronicles say his right hand was cut off, but a miniature in the Radziwiłł Chronicle shows the left arm, and an examination of his exhumed body in 1965 found many cut marks on the left humerus and forearm bones. His death set off a war of succession in Suzdalia from 1174 to 1177. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> canonised him in 1702. The icon Theotokos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogolyubovo">Bogolyubovo</a> was painted in the 12th century at his request.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrey Bogolyubsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1169 <b>Andrei Bogolyubsky</b> of Vladimir-Suzdal sacked Kiev and gave it to a junior relative rather than ruling there himself, a sign that the political centre had shifted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1170 the city repelled a siege by the forces of <b>Andrey Bogolyubsky</b> of Vladimir-Suzdal, a victory later attributed to the miraculous Icon of the Sign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The icon of Our Lady of Vladimir, painted in Constantinople in the early 12th century, was brought north by <b>Andrei Bogolyubsky</b> in 1155 and is now in Moscow’s Tretyakov Gallery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bulgars besieged Suzdal in 1107, and <b>Andrei Bogolyubsky</b> led a major campaign against them in 1164, taking the town of Bryakhimov.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The armies of <b>Andrei Bogolyubsky</b> sacked it in 1169, and Rurik Rostislavich and his Cuman allies sacked it again in 1203.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kiev">Principality of Kiev</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Andrei Bogolyubsky</b> it became a separate appanage of the Vladimir-Suzdal lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-pereslavl-zalessky">Principality of Pereslavl-Zalessky</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rostov Veliky, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Bogolyubovo, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.18,39.42&amp;year=1151" aria-label="Rostov Veliky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5398 225 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrei Bogolyubsky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5848 495h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5848 495h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.18,39.42&amp;year=1151">What was here in 1151? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
